%description:

RFC 2131 -- with nothing to answer it, a client repeats its DHCPDISCOVER, and the interval
between the repetitions grows.

Checks RFC2131-RETX-1 (must) and RETX-3 (description) from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc2131/catalog.md. The English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/dhcp/checks/retransmission.md#discover-repeated-without-a-server;
the program steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the mockup with no server:
client C = hostA, host E = hostE.

Defect: DhcpClient waits a constant time for an answer. The value is a
fixed 60 seconds -- "responseTimeout = 60; // response timeout in seconds RFC 2131, 4.4.3",
src/inet/applications/dhcp/DhcpClient.cc:45 -- and scheduleTimerTO reschedules that same value
every time, at :674. So the intervals between the repetitions are 60 seconds each and never grow.
RFC 2131 section 4.1 makes the growth a MUST: "The client MUST adopt a retransmission strategy
that incorporates a randomized exponential backoff algorithm to determine the delay between
retransmissions." Steps 0 to 2 match and step 3 fails. The assertion stays as the standard states
it; see results.md.

The 60 seconds the model uses is the figure RFC 2131 section 4.4.3 names for how long a client
should wait in total before it tells the user, which is a different quantity from the delay
between two retransmissions that section 4.1 is about.

The check states no bound on the instants of the repetitions, on purpose. Any bound would be a
bound on a distribution, and a run that drew an unlucky value would then fail for the wrong
reason. The one thing a single run can say is that the second interval is longer than the first.

This test FAILS, and the failure is deliberately NOT declared expected. The model claims this
behaviour: it has code for the delay between retransmissions -- responseTimeout at
src/inet/applications/dhcp/DhcpClient.cc:45 and scheduleTimerTO at :674 -- and that code puts a
constant where RFC 2131 section 4.1 demands a randomized exponential law. A mechanism that is
present with the wrong law is a defect, not a missing feature, so nothing declares this failure
expected. See results.md.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "DhcpChecks.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// A rolling record of the DHCPDISCOVER messages seen so far: the instant of the last one and
// the interval that preceded it. The growth test compares two **consecutive** intervals, so the
// record must advance on every message the predicate sees and not only on the one that matches.
// A record that advanced only on a match would compare the newest interval against a stale one,
// and a constant stream of repetitions would then look like a growing one.
static simtime_t lastAt;
static simtime_t lastInterval;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    lastAt = -1;
    lastInterval = -1;
    return ProtocolTest("rfc2131_discover_retransmission")
        // Expected observation 1: the first DHCPDISCOVER; record its instant.
        .once(on("hostA.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      if (!isDhcp(c.event.packet, DHCPDISCOVER))
                          return false;
                      lastAt = c.event.time;
                      return true;
                  })
                  .describe("the first DHCPDISCOVER from the client")
                  .within(5.0))
        // Expected observation 2: nothing answers it. The absence covers the window up to the
        // first repetition; a DHCPOFFER cannot arrive at all, because no node of the mockup sends
        // one.
        .never(on("hostA.udp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return isDhcp(c.event.packet, DHCPOFFER); })
                  .describe("a DHCPOFFER reaching the client")
                  .within(59.0))
        // Expected observation 3: a second DHCPDISCOVER; record its instant.
        .once(on("hostA.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      if (!isDhcp(c.event.packet, DHCPDISCOVER))
                          return false;
                      lastInterval = c.event.time - lastAt;
                      lastAt = c.event.time;
                      return true;
                  })
                  .describe("a second DHCPDISCOVER from the client, which fixes the first interval")
                  .within(120.0))
        // Expected observations 4 and 5: a third DHCPDISCOVER, and the second interval is longer
        // than the first.
        .once(on("hostA.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      if (!isDhcp(c.event.packet, DHCPDISCOVER))
                          return false;
                      simtime_t interval = c.event.time - lastAt;
                      bool grew = lastInterval >= 0 && interval > lastInterval;
                      lastInterval = interval;
                      lastAt = c.event.time;
                      return grew;
                  })
                  .describe("a later DHCPDISCOVER whose interval from the one before it is longer than the interval that preceded that one")
                  .within(240.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc2131_discover_retransmission compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The mockup with no server: a client and a far end that runs no DHCP.
network Rfc2131NoServerNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator {
            config = xml("<config><interface hosts='hostE' address='192.168.1.9' netmask='255.255.255.0'/></config>");
            @display("p=100,50");
        }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        hostE: StandardHost { @display("p=450,100"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> hostE.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc2131NoServerNet
sim-time-limit = 400s
seed-set = 0

**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

*.hostA.numApps = 1
*.hostA.app[0].typename = "DhcpClient"
*.hostA.app[0].interface = "eth0"

# hostE is the far end of the link. It runs no DHCP, so nothing can answer the client.
*.hostE.numApps = 0

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc2131_discover_retransmission: PASS
